%[text] %[text:anchor:T_DABTHERM] # Analyze Dual Active Bridge Temperature Rise with the Custom MOSFET Block
%[text] This design solution simulates the junction temperature of the SiC MOSFET devices in a dual active bridge converter that uses the custom Simulink-interfaced MOSFET Model block from `ConverterLibrary`, and estimates the device loss and the efficiency with improved simulation speed.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_scope] ## Design Solution Scope
%[text] In this design solution, you can:
%[text] - Specify the power stage, heatsink, device and controller data
%[text] - Set the operating point
%[text] - Estimate the device loss and the efficiency
%[text] - Simulate and plot the junction temperature rise of every bridge device \
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_sys] ## System Overview
%[text] The model comprises the DAB Converter plant and the DAB Controller. Each bridge device is a MOSFET Model block with its own heatsink.
modelName = "DABThermalAnalysisWithCustomMOSFET";
open_system(modelName);
%[text] ![](text:image:5d64)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_inputs] ## Specify the Input Parameters
%[text] `DABThermalAnalysisWithCustomMOSFETInputParameters` sets the power stage, heatsink, device and controller parameters. To open it, enter this command in the MATLAB® Command Window:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] edit 'DABThermalAnalysisWithCustomMOSFETInputParameters'
%[text] ```
DABThermalAnalysisWithCustomMOSFETInputParameters;
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_op] ## Define the Operating Point
%[text] Set the operating point below. The saved model is not changed.
inputVoltage       = simscape.Value(750, "V");     % DC input voltage (Vin)
outputVoltage      = simscape.Value(525, "V");     % Output-voltage setpoint (Vref)
loadPower          = simscape.Value(8000, "W");   % Delivered output power (Pload)
ambientTemperature = simscape.Value(25, "degC");   % Ambient temperature
simulationTimeSpan = simscape.Value(50e-3, "s");   % Simulated time span
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_sim] ## Simulate the Model
%[text] The `simulateDABThermalRise` function sets the required initial conditions and simulates the temperature rise.
thermalResult = simulateDABThermalRise(modelName, Vin = inputVoltage, Vref = outputVoltage, ...
    Pload = loadPower, AmbientTemperature = ambientTemperature, SimTime = simulationTimeSpan);
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_results] ## Plot the Switching and Conduction Loss
%[text] The `estimateDABDeviceLoss` function estimates the conduction and switching loss of every bridge device from the simulation. The pie chart shows how the total loss divides between the two bridges and the passive components, and the efficiency.
lossData = estimateDABDeviceLoss(thermalResult);
plotDABDeviceLoss(lossData); %[output:a41c07e2]
plotDABLossPieChart(lossData); %[output:b52d18f3]
%[text] ## 
%[text] ## Plot the Junction Temperatures
%[text] The plot shows the primary bridge devices in the left column and the secondary bridge devices in the right column along with the power loss summary.
powerFlowTable = displayDABPowerFlow(thermalResult, DisplayTableFlag = true); %[output:c63e2904]
plotDABDeviceTemperature(thermalResult.SimulationLog); %[output:d74f3a15]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_concl] ## Conclusion
%[text] This design solution estimated the device loss and the efficiency of a dual active bridge converter and simulated the junction temperature rise of every bridge device using the custom MOSFET block.
